README-GCC -- using the gcc toolchains on this diskFour GNU C toolchains are here, and every one of them compiles, linksand RUNS a C program on this disk -- verified 2026-08-24 for GCC2 andGCC139 and 2026-09-24 for GCC137 and GCC272.  None works out of thebox, and this file is the difference.WHICH ONE -- THE SHORT ANSWER  Take GCC272 unless memory is short.  It is GNU C 2.7.2, the newest here  by two years, it compiles C and C++, and it is the only one of the four  whose C++ is newer than 1992.  Take GCC137 if you want to read or rebuild the compiler you run.  It is  GNU C 1.37.1, C only, the smallest of the four, and its complete OS-9  source is on this disk.  GCC2 (2.5.6) and GCC139 (1.39) are the two older builds that have always  been here.  Their source is not, and they cannot both be installed at  once -- see WHAT TO DO.  GCC139 is the smallest C++ here, 891 KB to  GCC272's 1613, and that is the case for it where memory is short.  All four compile ordinary C from the era this disk collects.  Code that  an older one compiles, a newer one compiles too; the reverse is not  true.THE HARD FACTS  Measured from the files: the C path is driver + preprocessor + compiler,  the C++ path is the C++ driver + preprocessor + C++ compiler.  `needs'  is the Microware run-time module a program uses, as DOC/INDEX stars it.                 version   C path   C++ path   source here   needs    GCC137       1.37.1     520 KB    (none)      whole       cio    GCC139       1.39       564 KB    891 KB      no          --    GCC2         2.5.6     1085 KB    922 KB      driver only --    GCC272       2.7.2     1289 KB   1613 KB      most        csl (collect)  GCC2's C++ is gpp 1.40.3 running the 1.40.3 cc1plus -- the 2.5.8 pass,  cc2plus, is in the directory but nothing forks it.  GCC139's C++ is  1.37.1.  Both predate templates and exceptions.  GCC137 and GCC272 were given module names of their own (gcc137_cc1,  gcc272_cc2 and so on), so either can be installed beside anything else.  GCC2 and GCC139 cannot share a system: see WHAT TO DO.WHAT IS HERE  /dd/CMDS/GCC272    GNU C 2.7.2, Stephan Paschedag's port, 1995-97.                     gcc272 drives C and C++; the passes are                     gcc272_cccp2, gcc272_cc2, gcc272_cc2plus and                     gcc272_collect.  It forks its passes by bare name, so                     point it at them with its own -F option, which the                     GCC2_OPTS variable can carry for every run:                       P=/dd/CMDS/GCC272                       GCC2_OPTS="-Fcccp2x[$P/gcc272_cccp2] -Fcc2x[$P/gcc272_cc2]                         -Fcc2plusx[$P/gcc272_cc2plus] -Fcollectx[$P/gcc272_collect]"                       gcc272 -uwlibs -L/h1/LIB -o hello hello.c                     -uwlibs links your OS-9's own clibn.l; the port's                     default C library, gclib, is built partly from                     Microware's objects and is not here.  /dd/CMDS/GCC137    GNU C 1.37.1, the CERN distribution of 1991.  gcc137                     drives C; the passes are gcc137_cccp and gcc137_cc1.                     -B names where they are, and the prefix includes the                     start of the FILE name:                       gcc137 -B/dd/CMDS/GCC137/gcc137_ -I/h1/DEFS                         -L/dd/GCCLIB -L/h1/LIB -o hello hello.c                     If GCC139's passes have run, `unlink cc1' first: they                     stay resident under the names cc1 and cccp, and gcc137                     asks for those before it looks where -B points.  /dd/CMDS/GCC2      GNU C 2.5.6.  gcc2 drives C, gpp drives C++.  The                     passes are cccp2, cc2, cc1plus, cc2plus and collect.  /dd/CMDS/GCC139    GNU C 1.39, the same shape, passes already named                     gcc_cc1, gcc_cccp, gcc_cc1plus, gcc_collect.  /dd/GNULIB         os9lib.l, gnulib.l, gpp.l, libgpp.l, and GCC272's                     libgcc000.l, libgcc020.l and libgcc300.l  /dd/GCCLIB         GCC137's gnulib.l  /dd/GCCDEFS        GCC137's own headers  /dd/DEFS/GCC2      the gcc header set  /dd/DEFS/os9lib    the C headers matching GNULIB/os9lib.l  /dd/DOC/gcc137     the 1.37.1 distribution's documents  /dd/DOC/gcc272     the 2.7.2 port's readme.oskTHE SOURCE FOR THESE BINARIES, AND WHERE IT IS NOT  GCC is under the GNU General Public Licence, which is about SOURCE: what  you receive, you may pass on, and whoever receives it should be able to  get the source.  So this file says plainly what is here and what is not.  HERE:    SRC/gcc-1.37.1-osk  the whole of GCC137, as distributed with the                        binaries.  README.OSK in it lists every change.    SRC/gccsrc272       GCC272's source, as released with it -- the                        driver, preprocessor, collector, libgcc and every                        target-independent pass.  It does NOT carry the                        OS-9 machine description or the C++ front end, so                        cc2 and cc2plus cannot be rebuilt from it alone;                        its README.OSK says exactly what is missing.    SRC/gcc142          the source of GCC2's gcc driver, which reports                        `gcc version 1.42' -- the OS-9 port of GNU C 1.42    SRC/vh              VH, the hypertext browser shipped as `jargon'  NOT HERE, and we have looked:    gcc 1.39 and its passes            CMDS/GCC139    g++ 1.40.3 (gpp, cc1plus)          CMDS/GCC2, CMDS/GCC139    gcc 2.5.6 (gcc2, cc2, cccp2)       CMDS/GCC2    cc2plus 2.5.8                      CMDS/GCC2  These arrived as binaries in the OS-9 archives that kept them, and no  copy of the matching source has been found in any archive this project  has been able to reach.  The 2.5.6 archive does at least ship the GPL  itself as `copying'; the 1.x archives carry no licence text at all.  Upstream GNU C 1.39, 1.40, 1.42 and 2.5.6 survive in the GNU archives;  what is missing is the OS-9 port of them.  If you have it, this  collection would like it.WHAT IS NOT HERE, AND CANNOT BE  r68 (assembler), l68 (linker), clibn.l and cstart.r are Microware's and  are not in this collection.  gcc compiles C to ASSEMBLY and then hands  that to r68, and hands the objects to l68.  Without those two you can  preprocess and compile and get no further.  So: you need your own OS-9 SDK -- r68, l68 and, for GCC272, del; and  the LIB and DEFS the examples above name as /h1/LIB and /h1/DEFS.  Nothing on this disk can replace it,  and the six assemblers here (as0, as09, as1, as4, as5, as11) will not  do -- they are 6800, 6809, 6801, 6804, 6805 and 68HC11 cross-assemblers,  not 68000 ones.HOW THE DRIVERS FIND THEIR PASSES -- THE SURPRISE  gcc2 and gpp do not look in the directory they were started from.  Each  has two prefixes compiled into it, and it looks ONLY there:      gcc2   /dd/CMDS/gcc_<pass>    and  /h0/CMDS/gcc_<pass>      gpp    /dd/CMDS/gpp_<pass>    and  /h0/CMDS/gpp_<pass>  Note that they are DIFFERENT prefixes.  Anything else a driver runs --  r68, l68, del -- it forks by bare name, which OS-9 looks up in the  EXECUTION directory, normally /dd/CMDS.  The passes ship in /dd/CMDS/GCC2, which is none of those places, so a  bare attempt stops immediately:      $ /dd/CMDS/GCC2/gcc2 -c hello.c      gcc2: Can't fork 'cccp2': 000:221 module not found  That is not a broken binary.  It is a program looking where it was told  to look and not finding anything.WHAT TO DO  1.  Put your SDK's r68 and l68 where a bare name is found -- /dd/CMDS,      or /h0/CMDS if you would rather keep this disk read-only.  2.  Nothing.  The five copies this used to ask you to make ARE NOW ON      THE DISK, installed 2026-08-27:          /dd/CMDS/gcc_cccp2      gcc2's preprocessor          /dd/CMDS/gcc_cc2        gcc2's C compiler proper          /dd/CMDS/gpp_cccp       the same preprocessor under gpp's name          /dd/CMDS/gpp_cc1plus    gpp's C++ compiler proper          /dd/CMDS/gpp_collect    gpp's global-constructor collector      cccp2 is present twice on purpose: gcc2 forks it as cccp2 and gpp      forks it as cccp.  The originals are still in /dd/CMDS/GCC2; these      are byte copies, so the two directories cannot disagree.      GCC139 is deliberately NOT installed this way.  Its passes are      already named gcc_cc1, gcc_cccp, gcc_cc1plus and gcc_collect, and      its gpp, like GCC2's, forks /dd/CMDS/gpp_cc1plus -- the two collide      there.  To use GCC139 instead, copy      /dd/CMDS/GCC139's passes into /dd/CMDS yourself and remove GCC2's.  3.  Put clibn.l and cstart.r where l68 will find them -- /dd/LIB or      /h0/LIB.  Then both of these work:      /dd/CMDS/GCC2/gcc2 -o hello hello.c      /dd/CMDS/GCC2/gpp  -o hello hello.cc  GCC139 cannot be installed at the same time as GCC2 this way: its  gcc_cc1plus and GCC2's would want the same name.  Pick one.HEADERS  <stdio.h> in /dd/DEFS/GCC2 forwards to /dd/DEFS/os9lib/stdio.h, which is  the header that matches GNULIB/os9lib.l, the C library this disk ships  for gcc.  It used to forward to /dd/DEFS/stdio.h, which is Microware's,  in your own OS-9's DEFS rather than this collection's, so nothing that  included <stdio.h> could be compiled at all.  Fixed 2026-08-24.  os9lib's header has no SEEK_SET, SEEK_CUR or SEEK_END; DEFS/GCC2/stdio.h  adds them.ONE COSMETIC FAILURE YOU WILL SEE  gpp removes its temporary files by forking `del', and this disk has rm  and no del.  You will see      gpp: Program del got fatal signal 221.  AFTER your program has already been written.  The output is fine; the  temporaries are not cleaned up.  Supply a del from your SDK if it  bothers you.